1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,593
Plenty of teams plan a product the way they plan a project.

2
00:00:03,813 --> 00:00:06,245
A start date, an end date, a launch party.

3
00:00:06,465 --> 00:00:08,855
And then the product quietly dies on schedule.

4
00:00:09,605 --> 00:00:11,439
Here is the difference in one line.

5
00:00:11,659 --> 00:00:13,238
A project is temporary.

6
00:00:13,458 --> 00:00:17,149
It has a start, an end, and a result that is defined up front.

7
00:00:17,369 --> 00:00:18,457
Build the website.

8
00:00:18,677 --> 00:00:19,914
Migrate the system.

9
00:00:20,134 --> 00:00:24,166
When the result is delivered, the project is over, and the team moves on.

10
00:00:24,916 --> 00:00:26,708
A product does not end at launch.

11
00:00:26,928 --> 00:00:30,277
It lives as long as it keeps delivering value to customers.

12
00:00:30,497 --> 00:00:32,844
Launch is just the point where you start learning.

13
00:00:33,064 --> 00:00:36,844
You watch what people actually use, you fix what they struggle with,

14
00:00:36,844 --> 00:00:39,123
and you keep deciding what to build next.

15
00:00:39,343 --> 00:00:42,031
The work continues until the product is retired,

16
00:00:42,031 --> 00:00:43,823
not when a plan says it is done.

17
00:00:44,573 --> 00:00:46,791
This changes the old project triangle.

18
00:00:47,011 --> 00:00:49,403
In a classic project, the scope is fixed,

19
00:00:49,403 --> 00:00:52,494
and time and cost are what move when things go wrong.

20
00:00:52,714 --> 00:00:55,018
That is why projects slip and overspend.

21
00:00:55,238 --> 00:00:57,115
In agile product work, it flips.

22
00:00:57,335 --> 00:01:00,322
Time and budget are set, usually in short cycles.

23
00:01:00,542 --> 00:01:03,273
Scope is what you adjust, based on what you learn.

24
00:01:03,493 --> 00:01:06,629
You cut the least valuable work instead of missing the date.

25
00:01:07,379 --> 00:01:09,000
So the questions change too.

26
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:10,705
A project manager asks:

27
00:01:10,705 --> 00:01:14,191
did we deliver what was agreed, on time and on budget?

28
00:01:14,411 --> 00:01:15,610
A product owner asks:

29
00:01:15,610 --> 00:01:19,893
is this still worth building for our customers, and what should we do next?

30
00:01:20,113 --> 00:01:21,521
Both questions matter.

31
00:01:21,741 --> 00:01:24,195
But only the second one keeps a product alive.

32
00:01:24,945 --> 00:01:27,782
The early history of PayPal is a good reminder.

33
00:01:28,002 --> 00:01:31,501
It started with a plan to move money between handheld devices.

34
00:01:31,721 --> 00:01:34,630
The part people actually used, paying by email,

35
00:01:34,630 --> 00:01:36,734
turned out to be the real product.

36
00:01:36,954 --> 00:01:39,429
A team locked into a fixed scope could have missed it.

37
00:01:40,179 --> 00:01:43,805
Picture a product information system rolled out as a project.

38
00:01:44,025 --> 00:01:47,908
The go-live happens, the project closes, and the team is reassigned.

39
00:01:48,128 --> 00:01:52,181
Six months later the product data is out of date, because nobody owns it.

40
00:01:52,401 --> 00:01:56,135
That is what treating a product like a project looks like in practice.

41
00:01:56,885 --> 00:01:58,463
Three things to do this week.

42
00:01:58,683 --> 00:01:58,923
One:

43
00:01:58,923 --> 00:02:02,097
name an owner for the outcome, not just the delivery.

44
00:02:02,317 --> 00:02:02,541
Two:

45
00:02:02,541 --> 00:02:06,178
set a review date after launch, and treat it as part of the work.

46
00:02:06,398 --> 00:02:06,792
Three:

47
00:02:06,792 --> 00:02:10,537
define success in customer terms, not in tasks completed.

48
00:02:10,757 --> 00:02:13,829
That is the shift from project thinking to product thinking.

49
00:02:14,579 --> 00:02:17,885
That was episode one of Product Management in Plain Language.

50
00:02:18,105 --> 00:02:18,776
Next time:

51
00:02:18,776 --> 00:02:22,735
how to tell simple, complicated and complex problems apart.

52
00:02:22,955 --> 00:02:24,597
Sources are in the description.
